# ThreadSanitizer reports a data race entirely inside OpenSSL's libcrypto
# when several concurrent client handshakes verify peer certificates against
# one shared trust store. chttpclient's Tier 2/3 async engine does exactly
# that whenever it has more than one request in flight to a
# certificate-verifying HTTPS origin (see
# async_tls.concurrent_https_requests_all_succeed in tests.c).
#
# The report always has the same shape: ASN1_STRING_cmp reads a heap block
# that ASN1_STRING_set writes at the same time, the block comes from
# CRYPTO_malloc, and every frame that touches it is inside libcrypto.so.3.
# In spite of the report every concurrent handshake completes and verifies
# correctly, so what the detector reports is the access pattern and not a
# failed handshake.
#
# The race belongs to libcrypto and not to us. A standalone program that
# links nothing but libssl and libcrypto, and runs eight concurrent
# socketpair-backed client handshakes against one shared client SSL_CTX,
# gives the identical ASN1_STRING_cmp/ASN1_STRING_set report with no
# application frame in either racing access stack.
#
# We can't use either mitigation on the calling side; both were measured
# against that same standalone program. Putting each client's whole
# handshake call behind one mutex does remove the report, but this module
# drives the handshake one non-blocking step at a time
# (ctls_conn_handshake_step, declared in include/internal/ctls.h), so the
# lock would span the network waits between steps and serialise the real
# data transfer of concurrent connections instead of a short CPU-bound
# moment. Giving every connection its own SSL_CTX, and so its own trust
# store, removes this report too, but it exposes a different
# libcrypto-internal race instead and costs a full trust-store rebuild per
# connection.
#
# The anchor names a libcrypto frame and never one of our own functions. A
# suppression matches on any frame of either racing access, so an anchor
# naming a function in this module would silence a race in our own code
# just as easily as the third-party one it's written for, and an anchor on
# the handshake-stepping call site would silence a race whose only frames
# are that function itself. Two things stop that from happening quietly.
# The check_tsan_supp target of the Makefile fails the build when an anchor
# below names a symbol that this test binary defines, and test_tsan runs
# with print_suppressions=1, so every run prints how many times the anchor
# matched and you can tell a dead suppression from a working one.
race:ASN1_STRING_cmp
